## 10. Practical Data Quality Assessment Example

### 10.1. Sample Dataset

The following sample records illustrate common data quality problems.

| Booking ID | Customer ID | Travel Date | Amount (SAR) | Status |
|---|---|---|---:|---|
| BK1001 | C001 | 2026-10-15 | 1,500 | Confirmed |
| BK1002 | C002 | Missing | 2,000 | Confirmed |
| BK1003 | C003 | 2026-10-18 | 1,800 | Cancelled |
| BK1003 | C003 | 2026-10-18 | 1,800 | Cancelled |
| BK1005 | C005 | Invalid Date | 1,200 | Confirmed |

### 10.2. Data Quality Findings

Completeness Issue

Booking BK1002 has a missing travel date.

Uniqueness Issue

Booking BK1003 appears twice.

Validity Issue

Booking BK1005 contains an invalid travel date.

### 10.3. Sample Calculations

Travel Date Completeness

Total records = 5

Records with populated travel dates = 4

Completeness = (4 / 5) × 100 = 80%

Booking Reference Uniqueness

Total records = 5

Records beyond the first occurrence of each booking reference = 1

Uniqueness = (4 / 5) × 100 = 80%

Travel Date Validity

Records with valid travel date values = 3

Validity = (3 / 5) × 100 = 60%

For this example, a missing value fails both the required-field completeness rule and the travel-date validity rule. In production, the denominator and treatment of missing values must be explicitly defined for each rule.

### 10.4. Corrective Actions

- Investigate and populate the missing travel date using an authoritative booking
source.

- Review the duplicated booking reference and remove or reconcile the redundant
record without losing legitimate transaction history.

- Correct the invalid travel date after verification.
- Introduce validation rules to prevent recurrence.
This example demonstrates how data quality dimensions can be converted into measurable operational controls.

