## 19. Lessons Learned

The case study highlights several important lessons.

1. Data quality requires business ownership.

Technology teams can implement controls, but business departments must define acceptable data quality requirements.

2. Data profiling should precede remediation.

Understanding the nature and extent of quality problems is essential before selecting corrective actions.

3. Root cause analysis is more valuable than repeated correction.

Preventing errors at their source reduces operational costs and improves long-term quality.

## 4. Critical Data Elements should receive priority.

Not all data requires the same level of monitoring or investment.

5. Automation improves consistency and sustainability.

Automated validation and monitoring reduce dependence on manual inspection.

6. Data quality must be linked to business outcomes.

The value of data quality improvement should be demonstrated through measurable operational, financial, and customer-related benefits.

